#{[17592186176472 :question/worked-solution "With $N(0)=150000$, the solution is $N(t)=150000e^{-0.05t}$. Reaching $50000$ particles requires\n$$\ne^{-0.05t}=\\frac13,\\qquad t=\\frac{\\ln3}{0.05}\\approx21.9722.\n$$\nRounded to the nearest year, this is **22 years**."] [17592186189634 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186189639] [17592186190978 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186190980] [17592186190984 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186190986] [17592186191026 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186191031] [17592186192183 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186192186] [17592186192958 :question/worked-solution "Treat $x$ and $y$ as constants and apply the chain rule:\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=y\\cdot\\frac12(x^2+z^2)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2z=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{x^2+z^2}}.\n$$\nThe formula holds when $x^2+z^2>0$. At $x=z=0$, this partial derivative exists only when $y=0$."] [17592186193182 :question/worked-solution "Treat $x$ as constant. The product rule gives\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=0+x\\left(\\sin y+y\\cos y\\right)=x(\\sin y+y\\cos y).\n$$"] [17592186193183 :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule while holding $y$ fixed,\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=\\frac{-(x+y)\\sin x-\\cos x}{(x+y)^2}.\n$$\nAt $(\\pi,0)$, the denominator is nonzero and\n$$\nf_x(\\pi,0)=\\frac{-\\pi\\sin\\pi-\\cos\\pi}{\\pi^2}=\\frac1{\\pi^2}.\n$$"] [17592186193190 :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule gives\n$$\nf_y=\\frac{(2ye^{y^2})(x^2-2y)-e^{y^2}(-2)}{(x^2-2y)^2}\n=\\frac{(2x^2y-4y^2+2)e^{y^2}}{(x^2-2y)^2}.\n$$\nThus $h(x,y)=2x^2y-4y^2+2$. The derivative is defined where $x^2-2y\\ne0$."] [17592186193376 :question/worked-solution "Treat $x$ as constant and apply the chain rule:\n$$\nf_y=e^{4x-x^4y}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(4x-x^4y)=-x^4e^{4x-x^4y}.\n$$"] [17592186193890 :tutorial/content "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot {\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}})\\cdot {\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})+3{\\color{blue}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{\\color{blue}x}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot {\\color{blue}x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2})\\cdot {\\color{blue}x}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186216441 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216443] [17592186216446 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216448] [17592186216448 :answer/value "\\exp (\\int g(x)dx)"] [17592186216449 :answer/value "\\exp (-\\int g(x)dx)"] [17592186216622 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216625] [17592186216627 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216629] [17592186216632 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216636] [17592186216637 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216639] [17592186216639 :answer/value "\\exp (\\int g(x)dx)"] [17592186216640 :answer/value "\\exp (-\\int g(x)dx)"] [17592186216641 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216642] [17592186216646 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216648] [17592186216651 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186216653]}
